    questionText: "For the Stieltjes integral \\( \\displaystyle F(x)=\\int_0^{\\infty}\\frac{e^{-t}}{1+xt}\\,dt \\), it is given that \\( F(x)\\sim\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}(-1)^n n!\\,x^{n} \\) as \\( x\\to 0^{+} \\). By differentiating under the integral sign, find \\( F'(x) \\) as an integral and hence its asymptotic series.",
    marks: 4,
    examStyle: true,
    yearCreated: 2026,
    tags: ["differentiate under integral", "Stieltjes integral", "asymptotic series"],
    workedSolution: {
      steps: [
        { stepNumber: 1, description: "Differentiate the integrand in \\( x \\).", workingLatex: "\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\frac{1}{1+xt} = -\\frac{t}{(1+xt)^2}.", explanation: "Treat \\(t\\) as a parameter and differentiate only in \\(x\\). The chain rule on \\((1+xt)^{-1}\\) brings down \\(-1\\cdot(1+xt)^{-2}\\) times the inner derivative \\(t\\)." },
        { stepNumber: 2, description: "Justify and apply Leibniz's rule.", workingLatex: "F'(x) = \\int_0^{\\infty}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\frac{e^{-t}}{1+xt}\\,dt = -\\int_0^{\\infty}\\frac{t\\,e^{-t}}{(1+xt)^2}\\,dt.", explanation: "Move \\(d/dx\\) inside the integral. This is Leibniz's rule, valid here because for \\(x>0\\) the integrand and its \\(x\\)-derivative are dominated by the integrable function \\(t\\,e^{-t}\\) uniformly on a neighbourhood of \\(x\\)." },
        { stepNumber: 3, description: "Differentiate the series term by term.", workingLatex: "\\frac{d}{dx}\\sum_{n\\ge 0}(-1)^n n!\\,x^n = \\sum_{n\\ge 1}(-1)^n n!\\,n\\,x^{n-1}.", explanation: "Differentiate \\((-1)^n n!\\,x^n\\) to \\((-1)^n n!\\,n\\,x^{n-1}\\). The \\(n=0\\) constant term differentiates to zero, so the sum now starts at \\(n=1\\)." },
        { stepNumber: 4, description: "Re-index to start from \\( x^0 \\).", workingLatex: "= \\sum_{m\\ge 0}(-1)^{m+1}(m+1)!\\,(m+1)\\,x^{m}.", explanation: "Put \\(m=n-1\\) so the powers run \\(x^0,x^1,\\dots\\). Then \\(n=m+1\\) gives \\(n!\\,n=(m+1)!\\,(m+1)\\) and the sign \\((-1)^n\\) becomes \\((-1)^{m+1}\\)." },
        { stepNumber: 5, description: "Pull out the overall sign.", workingLatex: "= -\\sum_{m\\ge 0}(-1)^{m}(m+1)(m+1)!\\,x^{m}.", explanation: "Factor \\((-1)^{m+1}=-(-1)^m\\) out of the sum. The coefficient \\((m+1)(m+1)!\\) is left in factored form; this series is precisely that of \\(-\\int_0^\\infty t e^{-t}(1+xt)^{-2}\\,dt\\) from step 2." },
        { stepNumber: 6, description: "State the result.", workingLatex: "F'(x) = -\\int_0^{\\infty}\\frac{t\\,e^{-t}}{(1+xt)^2}\\,dt \\sim -\\sum_{m\\ge 0}(-1)^{m}(m+1)(m+1)!\\,x^{m}.", explanation: "The two routes agree: the differentiated series is asymptotic to \\(F'\\) because \\(F'\\) is smooth and itself Stieltjes-type, so term-by-term differentiation is legitimate for this family. The relation \\(F'=G\\) is exploited again in later questions." },
      ],
      finalAnswer: "\\( \\displaystyle F'(x) = -\\int_0^{\\infty}\\frac{t\\,e^{-t}}{(1+xt)^2}\\,dt \\sim -\\sum_{m\\ge 0}(-1)^{m}(m+1)(m+1)!\\,x^{m} \\).",
      canonicalAnswer: "-Integral(t*exp(-t)/(1+x*t)^2,(t,0,oo))",
    },
